high|[high] Add an admin emergency pause that never blocks refunds|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|The registry has no way to stop new leases if the escrow token or a dependency turns out to be unsafe, but a naive pause could also trap consumer funds.|Add a paused flag and decide precisely which entry points it blocks;Settlement and consumer revocation must remain available while paused;Emit a typed event for every pause and unpause and test both transitions and every blocked path|The consumer refund path is the protocol's strongest guarantee
high|[high] Make admin transfer a two-step, accept-based operation|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|The admin is set once by `init` and can never be changed, so a lost or rotated admin key permanently loses upgrade and token configuration without any recovery path.|Add a propose-and-accept admin transfer that requires auth from both the current and the new admin;Let the current admin cancel a pending transfer and expire stale proposals;Emit events for propose, cancel and accept and test that a third party can never take over|Stellar account auth makes multisig admins possible but not rotation
high|[high] Put a time lock on contract upgrades|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|The admin can install a new Wasm in the same transaction it is uploaded, so users have no window to exit a deployment before its rules change.|Add a propose-and-execute upgrade flow with a documented delay and a cancel path;Record the pending Wasm hash and execution ledger in storage and expose them through a view;Test that execution before the delay fails and that the event reports the hash and ledger|Upgrades preserve storage, which makes a delay practical
high|[high] Version the storage schema and reject unsafe upgrades|`contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`|Upgrades assume `DataKey` variants and stored value shapes never change, but nothing records which schema a deployment is running or checks it after an upgrade.|Store a schema version in instance storage and check it in `init` and after an upgrade;Define how a migration is proposed, reviewed and executed with the contract stopped or paused;Add tests that an incompatible schema version is rejected and document the procedure|`DataKey` is documented in `docs/contract-reference.md`
high|[high] Support topping up or renewing a lease|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/types.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|A consumer that needs more calls or time must create a second lease, which creates extra ids, extra rent and two settlement paths for one logical session.|Add a top-up entry point that increases calls or duration using the lease's original rate snapshot;Define exactly how the deposit is recomputed and how a partially used lease is handled;Emit a typed event and cover checked math, TTL extension and settlement after renewal|In-flight leases must never be repriced
high|[high] Add a bounded batch settlement entry point|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|Providers settle leases one transaction at a time, which is costly at scale and makes a queue of hundreds of leases a long operational tail.|Add a batch settle call with a hard maximum batch size and an explicit all-or-nothing or partial-success contract;Require provider auth and validate every lease before any transfer;Test the limit, duplicate ids, mixed ownership and the emitted events|No unbounded loops over user-supplied collections
high|[high] Replace direct payouts with a pull-payment claim model|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|Settlement and revocation transfer tokens inline, so a token failure can block settlement and every money path must reason about re-entrancy and transfer ordering.|Record each beneficiary's claimable balance during settle and revoke and let them withdraw separately;Keep settlement idempotent and make withdrawal checks-effects-interactions;Test re-entrancy, repeated withdrawal, zero balances and conservation of value|The current design already persists state before transfers
high|[high] Support optional signed usage receipts|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/types.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|The contract trusts the provider's `verified_calls` report entirely, so consumers rely only on the allotment and refund path rather than any attestation.|Design an optional per-service mode where settlement includes a provider-signed usage receipt bound to the lease id and service;Verify the signature on-chain with a documented message domain and reject replayed receipts;Keep the current mode as the default and test valid, invalid, replayed and mismatched signatures|Consumers are currently protected by the fixed allotment, not by proof of delivery
high|[high] Formally check the escrow state machine and conservation law|`contracts/registry/src/test.rs`, `docs/security.md`|Randomised testing is proposed separately, but there is no formal model proving that every reachable state conserves value or that settled is terminal.|Write a small formal model of the create, settle and revoke transitions with their guards;Check conservation, no-double-release and terminal-settled as invariants and keep the model in the repository;Document any assumption the model makes and how the implementation is kept in sync|Complements, rather than replaces, property-based testing
high|[high] Handle or explicitly reject fee-on-transfer tokens|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`, `docs/security.md`|The registry credits `locked_deposit` after calling `transfer`, assuming the contract receives exactly that amount, which is false for a token that deducts a fee.|Decide whether to support such tokens with balance-delta accounting or reject them using a documented balance check at configuration or first deposit;Cover the decision with an adversarial token test;Update the trust model and token-selection docs to state the guarantee|The admin picks the token, so this is also a configuration hazard
high|[high] Add per-service operator roles for settlement|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|Only the service's provider address can settle, so an operator cannot run settlement without holding the provider key that also controls registration and activation.|Add an owner-managed operator set with a bounded size and explicit grant and revoke entry points;Allow an authorized operator to settle but not to change ownership or rates;Emit events and test that a revoked operator immediately loses settlement rights|A future service transfer must define what happens to operators
high|[high] Introduce a capped, configurable protocol fee|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|The protocol takes no fee, so there is no sustainable way to fund development, audits or indexer infrastructure from settlement volume.|Add a fee in basis points with a hard cap and a configurable recipient;Split every settlement between provider, fee recipient and consumer refund without breaking conservation;Test rounding, the cap, zero fee and that the consumer's protected refund still holds|Decide whether the fee applies to revoked leases
high|[high] Add a consumer challenge window before settlement pays out|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|A consumer cannot dispute the provider's reported call count, so settlement is final even if the provider over-reports within the allocated maximum.|Design a challenge window in which a consumer can flag a settlement with a reason or evidence hash;Keep the payout locked until the window closes and define who resolves a challenge;Test the window boundary, cancellation and the no-challenge fast path|This is a protocol change, not just an entry point
high|[high] Allow revenue splits across multiple providers|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/types.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|A service has exactly one provider address, so teams that share operational and infrastructure costs must route payments off-chain.|Add a bounded split list in basis points that always sums to ten thousand;Pay each recipient during settlement with a bounded loop and a clear rounding rule;Emit the split in an event and test invalid sums, rounding and a single-recipient service|Keep the existing single-provider layout as the default
high|[high] Make leases transferable with consumer authorization|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|A lease is permanently bound to the consumer that created it, so a consumer cannot hand a prepaid lease to another account or sell unused access.|Add a transfer entry point authorized by the current consumer and accepted by or assigned to the new consumer;Move the refund right atomically and emit an event that indexers can follow;Test unauthorized transfer, transfer of a settled lease and settlement after transfer|Consider how this interacts with a challenge window
high|[high] Support early consumer cancellation with a defined refund|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/types.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|A consumer that stops using a service must wait until expiry plus the full grace window to recover anything, even if the provider agrees to release funds.|Design an early-cancel path with either provider consent or a configurable penalty that compensates the provider;Compute the refund from unused calls with checked arithmetic and define rounding;Test consent, refusal, cancellation after partial settlement and full conservation|The current exit is deliberately unconditional but slow
high|[high] Add optional oracle attestation for metered usage|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/types.rs`, `docs/security.md`|The provider alone reports consumed calls, and an optional attestation service could strengthen settlement without changing the default trust model.|Define an attestor interface, the attestation payload and how it binds to a lease id and service;Allow a service to opt in and reject settlement that lacks a valid attestation;Test a missing, stale, mismatched and valid attestation and document the added trust assumption|Keep the no-attestor mode fully supported
high|[high] Add provider staking and slashing for proven over-reporting|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/errors.rs`|A provider that over-reports within `allocated_calls` faces no economic penalty beyond consumer exit, because the contract cannot observe real usage.|Design an opt-in stake that a service must post before accepting leases;Define the evidence and authority that can slash a stake after a proven over-report;Test stake deposit, withdrawal limits, slashing and the effect on settlement and service deactivation|This needs a clear governance model before implementation
high|[high] Add a circuit breaker for anomalous payout volume|`contracts/registry/src/lib.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/storage.rs`, `contracts/registry/src/events.rs`|A compromised provider key or a pricing bug can drain a large amount of escrow across many leases before a human notices, even though each individual payout is bounded.|Add a configurable per-ledger or rolling-window payout cap that pauses settlements when exceeded;Keep consumer revocation available and define how the cap is raised or reset;Test the boundary, the paused state, the event and recovery|The cap must not be bypassable by splitting one lease into several
high|[high] Write the mainnet deployment and key-custody runbook|`docs/deployments.md`, `docs/security.md`, `scripts/`, `SECURITY.md`|Mainnet is intentionally deferred, but there is no concrete checklist covering audit prerequisites, multisig custody, deployment verification, monitoring and rollback.|Define the audit and review gate that must pass before any mainnet transaction;Specify a multisig admin and provider setup with named roles and recovery steps;Include deploy, verify, monitor and upgrade procedures with the exact commands and expected outputs|Testnet deployment is documented in `docs/deployments.md`
